diagnosis_text:
A complete diagnostic pass across all layers found no active or historical fault. At the infrastructure layer (Phase A), every device has correct IP addressing, default routes, and a clean ARP cache; no ACL/nftables rules block any protocol; no duplicate MAC addresses exist; all interfaces are UP. At the control-plane layer (Phase B), all six FRR routers are healthy: ospfd is running on each, all OSPF neighbour relationships are Full/DR or Full/Backup, and the routing table on every device carries the expected ten prefixes. Traffic-control inspection of all 30 interfaces shows only the default fq_codel qdisc — there is no netem delay, netem loss, or tbf rate limiter anywhere on the path to dns_server (10.200.0.2). At the service layer (Phase D), the DNS server answers every name correctly and promptly (≈4 ms end-to-end), and HTTP responses from all four web servers are HTTP 200 with sub-5 ms total times from both host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1. The dns_client_snapshot confirms identical, correct A-record answers from both hosts with no resolver outlier. The pressure_sweep finds no stress or flood tool, no CPU spike, and no socket exhaustion on the DNS server or any other device. The dhcp_link_history shows zero link-flap or DHCP-churn events. Because no mechanism capable of producing intermittent DNS slowness is present and no historical trace of such an event exists, the helpdesk report is unconfirmed. Root cause: there is no current or recent fault; the DNS server is healthy and no device should be restarted. Fix: none required — monitor live traffic if the symptom recurs to capture a contemporaneous trace.
=== END ===